/*
[bifurcation]
parameter: p1
min: 0
max: 1
ds: 0.01
dsMax: 0.02
maxSteps: 600
*/

// PP2, the AUTO-07p demo, transcribed from demos/pp2/pp2.f90 and c.pp2.
//
//     fish'   = p2 fish (1 - fish) - fish sharks - p1 (1 - exp(-p3 fish))
//     sharks' = -sharks + p4 fish sharks
//
// with p2 = 3, p3 = 5, p4 = 3, and p1 - the HARVESTING QUOTA, AUTO calls it
// `quota` - the continuation parameter over [0, 1]. AUTO names the states
// `fish` and `sharks`; PAR(1..4) there are p1..p4 here, in the same order,
// so a run can be laid beside AUTO's output without translating anything.
//
// The last of specification section 11.2's six validation models. It is
// there for the AUTOMATIC DIAGRAM of section 8 - its tree structure is what
// AUTO's published output is compared against - so it is the model stage 6
// will be judged on. Nothing here exercises stage 6 yet, because stage 6 is
// not written; what it does exercise now is the branch structure that
// diagram would have to find.
//
// WHY IT HAS THAT STRUCTURE. The origin is an equilibrium for every p1: the
// harvesting term p1(1 - exp(-p3 fish)) vanishes with fish, so
// fish = sharks = 0 solves both equations identically. That trivial branch
// runs the whole length of the parameter range and sheds NON-TRIVIAL
// branches at the parameter values where one of its eigenvalues crosses
// zero. A diagram of this model is therefore a tree rather than a single
// curve, which is the point.
//
// A PURE ODE MODEL, one source reaction per state. `fish` goes NEGATIVE on
// part of the diagram - AUTO's own c.pp2 stops the run at fish = -0.25 - so
// this is not a concentration and must not be read as one.
//
// Start it at the origin explicitly. A steady-state solve from the declared
// initial values is not wrong, but the trivial branch is the one the
// structure hangs off and `--start 0,0` says so.
//
// EVERY BIFURCATION ON THE FIRST TWO BRANCHES HAS A CLOSED FORM, which is
// what makes this model worth more than a tree to look at. On the trivial
// branch the Jacobian is diagonal - diag(p2 - p1 p3, -1) - so its spectrum
// is exact; on the fish-only branch sharks = 0 leaves a scalar equation, and
// the coexistence branch has fish pinned at 1/p4, which leaves another.
// Computed against the library:
//
//     BP  trivial branch        p1 = 3/5                    = 0.600000000000
//     NS0 trivial branch        p1 = (p2-1)/p3              = 0.400000000000
//         (a NEUTRAL SADDLE, both eigenvalues real - not a Hopf, and the
//          library says so)
//     BP  fish-only branch      p1 = (2/3)/(1-exp(-5/3))    = 0.821904345374
//     LP  fish-only branch                                    0.832929322234
//     H   coexistence branch                                  0.671593847479
//         supercritical, omega = 0.604782221942, period 10.389169984
//
// bifurcata returns 0.6000000023, 0.4000000023, 0.8219043458, 0.8329293222
// and 0.6715938423 - nine or ten significant figures on every one.
//
// THE DIAGRAM IS THREE LEVELS DEEP: trivial -> fish-only -> coexistence,
// and the Hopf is on the third. `--switch` goes ONE level, so it reaches the
// fish-only branch and the coexistence branch has to be started by hand,
// with --start. Reaching it automatically is stage 6, which is what this
// model is really here for.
//
// Suggested runs:
//     bifurcata run pp2.ant --param p1 --range 0:1 --start 0,0
//         the trivial branch: the branch point at 0.6 and the neutral
//         saddle at 0.4
//     bifurcata run pp2.ant --param p1 --range 0:1 --start 0,0 --switch
//         and the fish-only branch that comes out of it, carrying the fold
//         at 0.8329 and a second branch point at 0.8219
//     bifurcata run pp2.ant --param p1 --range 0:1 --start 0.33333333333333,2
//         the coexistence branch and its Hopf at 0.6716
//     bifurcata cycles pp2.ant --param p1 --range 0:1
//               --start 0.33333333333333,2
//         the cycles growing out of that Hopf

model pp2
    -> fish;    p2*fish*(1 - fish) - fish*sharks - p1*(1 - exp(-p3*fish));
    -> sharks;  -sharks + p4*fish*sharks;

    p1 = 0;
    p2 = 3;
    p3 = 5;
    p4 = 3;

    fish   = 0;
    sharks = 0;
end
